(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110000-six-defects-in-the-mass-rename-and-how-to-reproduce-each")
  (:adr/topic "mass-rename-defects-and-their-reproductions")
  (:adr/title "Six defects in the .clj to .kotoba mass rename, each with the command that reproduces it")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609109400-kotoba-form-dag-kotoba-and-what-guest-and-host-actually-name"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

The owner instruction of 2026-09-10 is: rename every .clj/.cljc/.cljs to
.kotoba across the workspace, tests included, and let the kotoba CLI say what
broke. Reader errors are fixed afterwards by grepping what the rename surfaced.

This ADR records the defects found in the driver that carries that out. It
records the REPRODUCTION for each, not the counts. Counts written into a
document here get quoted next week with the date dropped, which is the failure
this workspace keeps warning about.

Eighteen defects have now been found in this one script. Twelve were recorded
earlier; this covers 13 through 18. The rate matters more than any single
defect: a bulk operation over thousands of repositories generates defect
classes faster than reading the script finds them, and all six below were found
by measuring a run rather than by review.

## Decision 13: the exclusion criterion must be derived, not enumerated

The exclusion list was a hand-written enumeration whose stated criterion was
that the repository fleet gate names a clj-family :entry by path. That
criterion is correct for what it names, because :entry is not additive -- there
is one path and renaming the file removes it. It is not the criterion the
exclusion exists for.

It is replaced by a measurement: parse every launchd plist, take the
repositories named in ProgramArguments, EnvironmentVariables and
WorkingDirectory -- chiefly as nbb --classpath orgs/<org>/<repo>/src entries --
and intersect with real repository slugs. Those repositories run on a SCHEDULE,
so renaming their sources means nbb stops resolving the namespace at the next
tick.

    nbb gen-live-exclusions.cljs sizes.tsv

SIZE IS THE WRONG PROXY, and this inverts the framing the investigation began
with. Among the unprotected repositories this surfaced, jp-go-gbiz-info carries
ONE clj-family file, jp-go-npb-kanpou two, secret-resolve six -- while
murakumo, the fleet CI itself, carries a few hundred. Any file-count floor high
enough to protect murakumo renames the other three and breaks
com.gftd.gbizinfo-refresh at its next tick. A size floor is not a weaker
version of this criterion; it is a different and wrong one.

The evidence floor is the part that makes the answer usable:

    nbb gen-live-exclusions.cljs /tmp/two-fake-slugs.tsv    -> exit 2, REFUSED
    nbb gen-live-exclusions.cljs sizes.tsv                  -> exit 0

An unreadable set of plists returns the same empty answer as a machine with no
scheduled jobs at all, and an empty exclusion set means rename everything. So
zero is REFUSED rather than clean. Note also that a strict plist parser refused
11 of 77 plists that plutil reads without complaint; every one of those 11
would have been silently dropped from the exclusion.

## Decision 14: .clj is a PREFIX of .cljc and .cljs

The reference rewriter and its detector were unanchored substring matches. For
a moved path X.clj, any text occurrence of X.cljc had its head rewritten and
kept the orphan letter behind, giving X.kotobac.

    printf 'see src/talent/facts.cljc' > R.md
    FROM=src/talent/facts.clj TO=src/talent/facts.kotoba perl rewrite.pl R.md

Before the fix R.md reads facts.kotobac. After it, R.md is unchanged and the
rewriter exits 1.

The collision guard could not have caught this. It inspects files that EXIST,
and the case that reproduced it is a README naming a .cljc file where only the
.clj exists. Two further parts were fixed alongside it. The pull request body
asserted that project files are NOT updated, which had become false, so on the
order of 1,550 pull requests would each have carried a false statement about
their own diff. And the reference count was computed but never emitted, so
rewrote 14 references and rewrote 0 were indistinguishable; it is emitted now,
and it varies across repositories.

## Decision 15: the rewriter must refuse binary files

grep -F matches a byte sequence anywhere, including inside compiled artefacts,
and perl -pi then rewrote those bytes in place. Measured on a .pyc carrying a
path in a docstring constant: the rewrite grew the file by two bytes without
updating the marshal length prefix in front of it.

    python3 -c 'import marshal; marshal.loads(open(P,\"rb\").read()[16:])'

That loads before the rewrite and raises ValueError, bad marshal data, after.
Same file, same loader, and it fails for the reason named. The rename is +3
bytes, so ANY length-prefixed container holding such a path corrupts the same
way: .pyc, zip and jar entries, .class, sqlite, protobuf descriptors. Git
records these as binary, so the corruption is INVISIBLE in the pull request
diff -- a reviewer sees a file listed as changed with no visible change.

Detection and rewrite are now a single perl pass that refuses binaries. That
also removed a second hazard: the previous pair used two different regex
engines, grep -E with a hand-rolled sed escape and perl quotemeta, so a path
containing plus, brace or pipe was escaped by one and not the other, and the
two could disagree about whether the file matched at all.

## Decision 16: a run must pin its own bytes

zsh reads a script INCREMENTALLY. It seeks back to the byte offset it had
reached and carries on. Editing the file while a pass runs therefore does not
take effect next run; the RUNNING pass begins executing the new bytes at an
offset chosen by the old layout.

    cat > v.zsh          # echo phase-1; sleep 2; echo phase-2
    zsh v.zsh &  sleep 0.5;  rewrite v.zsh;  wait

The unguarded run dies with an unmatched-quote error, and v.zsh parses cleanly
as a whole file -- which is what proves the death is offset-driven rather than
a syntax error anyone could have seen. This is the exact reported shape of five
slice deaths that had no established cause: harness exit 1, a zero-byte run
log, and a trailing echo that never ran, meaning the shell died rather than the
script exiting.

The cause was the author of the script editing it while slices were running,
twice. A slice agent recorded the script sha256 at launch and found a different
one at exit, having never edited it.

A run now snapshots itself: the first invocation copies the script to a
read-only file named with timestamp and pid, re-execs from it, and records the
snapshot path and its sha256 in the ledger. The snapshot is kept rather than
cleaned up, because it is the only record of what a given ledger was produced
by.

## Decision 17: one ledger record is one line

On failure gh writes multi-line error text, and it landed in the sha field
unaltered, so a FAIL record spanned several ledger lines. Resume was never
affected, because continuation lines do not begin with a slug and a tab, but
the ledger stopped being one-record-per-line, and one-record-per-line is the
only property that makes it countable.

## Decision 18: an archived repository is a category, not a failure

Three repositories are archived on GitHub, so push and pull-request creation
are both read-only refusals. Recording that as FAIL sends a reader after
recoverable work that cannot exist. It is now recorded as ARCHIVED, and the
question is asked only on the failure path, so it costs one API call per
failure rather than one per repository.

## Consequences

Every one of these six was found by measuring a run, and none by reading the
script. Four share one shape this workspace already names: the failing case
returns the same value as the succeeding case. An unreadable plist set returns
an empty exclusion; a rewrite that silently does nothing returns the same exit
as one that worked; a corrupted binary shows an empty diff; a zsh-killed run
leaves a ledger that resume reads as merely incomplete.

One instruction is withdrawn and recorded as withdrawn. Slice agents had been
told not to re-measure before running. That was wrong: decisions 13, 15, 16, 17
and 18 were all found through exactly that channel, by agents measuring what
they had been told to take on trust.")))
